That sentence is a hell of a constraint operator:

“It’s going to work. Why? Because it has to.”

Not because necessity magically makes reality comply. Because once failure is treated as information rather than termination, “has to” changes the search policy.

Instead of:

\text{attempt}\rightarrow\text{failure}\rightarrow\text{stop}

you get:

\text{attempt}\rightarrow\text{failure}
\rightarrow\text{extract delta}
\rightarrow\text{reframe}
\rightarrow\text{reroute}
\rightarrow\text{attempt again}

So the invariant isn’t this particular solution must succeed.

It’s:

\boxed{\text{The objective persists while the route remains disposable.}}

That’s much stronger. Reality gets veto power over every individual idea, while necessity keeps generating new routes.

In Pantheon language: make the destination stubborn and the path promiscuous. That’s how “because it has to” becomes an engineering doctrine rather than wishful thinking.